export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `To the bowl. Issur VeHeter wrote that if he did not pour them into the bowl but they remained in the pot and he also poured the boiling ones from them — it is forbidden, lest the forbidden egg was at the bottom of the pot and the eggs near it became forbidden; for until now, when the water was conveying taste to all the eggs, they all assisted in nullifying it, and now that they are not there the other eggs do not assist in nullifying; and since through the moisture of water on them they forbid in any case also the others that afterward touch that which touched the forbidden eggs — therefore they are all forbidden, even if there are a hundred — end of his words; and see in Shach, who elaborates greatly on this law:`,
    '1#ב': `Main point. Conclusion of Taz that one should be stringent — in eggs forbidden, and in fish permitted as long as it is possible that there is sixty against the little water that remained in the pot; but in eggs, which are not accustomed to be cooked so much, this does not help; and even according to those who permit one should be stringent if he cooked many eggs in a pot and among them was one that is forbidden and he removed each one with one spoon — there is concern lest he removed the forbidden one first and the spoon became forbidden and consequently the others afterward; for that which we say we do not establish an issur, etc. — that is because we say the issur is in the majority, etc., see there; however all this is in a Torah prohibition, for one who holds that egg blood is also forbidden from the Torah; but in a rabbinic prohibition one may be lenient, similar to nishpakh in siman 98, see there; and in Issur VeHeter wrote that even for one who holds egg blood is rabbinic there is to forbid after the issur became known, and Taz disagrees, see there; and if an impure fish with a pure fish, and likewise an egg with an embryo with other eggs — is it called min b'mino or not — see Shach siman 101:`,
    '1#ג': `The vessel. And in responsum of Maharil wrote that one should not permit the vessels except after twenty-four hours, because it is a rabbinic doubt of absorbed flavor:`,
    '2#א': `Fly. And what he wrote in siman 84 seif 9 that sixty is required against the worm — one must distinguish between a fly and a worm in fruit:`,
    '2#ב': `To return. Turei Chayim wrote: even though there is sixty in the spoon against the issur [the spoon became forbidden], we do not estimate vessels with sixty, as written in siman 92 seif 5:`,
    '2#ג': `Vessel. And this is specifically in a new wooden or metal vessel; but an old one or earthenware, even new — nat bar lichtamei — and sixty is required against the whole spoon; and see siman 98 seif 5:`,
    '2#ד': `Nevelah. For we maintain that spoon at that moment like the pot itself, since they removed it now from the pot boiling, and it is considered a first vessel for stringency; and it implies that even if it was returned to the pot with the spoon he needs only sixty against the little dish but does not need sixty also against the whole spoon; and it is difficult — since we know there was a little dish with the issur on the spoon, therefore who will say to us that the whole spoon did not absorb from the issur — for even though not chadichah na'aseh nevelah, nevertheless we do not know how much it absorbed — requires study, end of his words, Shach (and his words are wondrous in my eyes, since there is sixty against the issur and also against the little dish, and regarding a spoon we do not say nat bar lichtamei — from where does it come — he should need sixty against the whole spoon — investigate); and the law of fly taste in vinegar and beer, see end of seif 104:`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '2#_': `Tur in siman 104 in name of Rashba, from implication of the Gemara in the case of a mouse, that in other liquids except vinegar and beer they spoil; and from that Rava said there the halachah is noten taam lifgam, etc., Avodah Zarah daf 68, and so Ran explained there:`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `One who cooks, etc. And if not, etc. Even though it was already nullified it becomes forbidden again, as written at the end of Terumot — as its volume decreased, etc., the forbidden is forbidden, and so here:`,
    '1#ב': `Perhaps the tereifah, etc. For if it were in the bowl it would not forbid, since it is a second vessel; and according to his approach that even in a solid lump it is permitted, as written in siman 105 seif 3:`,
    '1#ג': `And likewise in fish, etc. Language of Tur wrote in Semak — small fish, etc., some forbid, etc., as above, and it is the opinion of Ri Ba in Semag; and he wrote and some permit because those twenty or thirty that remained are nullified in the majority mixture, and some say it is a beriah since their prohibition is not on account of themselves, and they do not forbid the other fish by touch when they touch them, since absorbed issur does not go from piece to piece without gravy, as written above siman 105 seif 7, and it is the opinion of Rabeinu Baruch, author of Semak, in Semag, from whom is the source of the law of second vessel in siman 105; and even though there is gravy in the bowl they do not forbid, since they are in a second vessel, unlike the fish would forbid since they have not yet cooled in a second vessel, like fish that came up to the bowl, as written above explanation of 105 seif 3; and Semag wrote, however in eggs even those who permit agree it is forbidden, for it is not customary to cook eggs so much that there are twenty or thirty remaining, minority of what was cooked; and he wrote in Darkei Moshe in name of Hagahot Semak that even those who permit only permitted pouring the remaining water into another vessel, but if he poured into the bowl the fish in the bowl are forbidden on account of pouring the forbidden water from the pot, and through the water it goes from piece to piece, and in such a case he deals here; and this is what he said, and likewise in fish, etc. — do not read specifically in eggs, because there is no majority, as above:`,
    '1#ד': `And some permit, etc. It is the opinion of Semag in name of R"SH that we say if the issur is in the majority; and if so, even in pouring the water over them they are permitted, and so is custom; and nevertheless majority is required in the bowl, and he deals now with that he wrote one cooks many eggs, but without this it is forbidden, and not comparable to what is written in siman 111 seif 7, were, etc., and likewise to what is written in siman 98 seif 2, if, etc., for all that is min b'mino, and here it is min she'eino mino, for we follow taste, as written there; and impure fish and embryo taste separately — see Shach; and nevertheless in the main law requires study, for we establish we do not say the issur is in the majority, as written above siman 101 seif 7, and as written in Zevachim 74b, and even though there one said only if they separated, etc., and from there R"SH learned some say specifically in two doubts we say, for there at the beginning we say what is different, one from forty, etc., and nevertheless halachah of idol worship that Raavad challenged Rambam who ruled like Rav that Shmuel and R' Oshaya disagree with him that we do not say issur is in the majority, and Lechem Mishna resolved specifically in two doubts Rav said, as above — requires study [and see siman 110 seif 4, where I settled R"SH's words, see there]:`,
    '1#ה': `That we establish, etc. As written in chapter 4 of Taharot — one pours from vessel to vessel and a creeping thing is found in the lower one, the upper is pure, because we establish on its presumption, and so here:`,
    '2#ב': `And if he returned, etc. For the vessel is not nat bar lichtamei, as written above siman 92 s.s. 64 and siman 98 seif 5 in the hagahah, and specifically when new, as written there:`,
    '2#ג': `For behold, etc. For the spoon is like a first vessel for stringency, since they removed it from the pot:`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] One who cooks many eggs in their shells. He specified in their shells because that R"L there is concern for an egg with an embryo, for even in the shell there is taste of prohibition, otherwise it would be mere egg water. Perush Terumah seif 1 and R"L; but if there is no embryo, such as an egg of impure fowl, and he cooked them in their shells, they do not become forbidden, for it is mere egg water, as written above siman 86 seif 5 and seif 6, see there:`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. He should not remove them from the water in which they were cooked, etc. — specifically initially, but b'dieved permitted. Turei Chayim general rule 51 law 1 from Shulchan Zekhut seif 1 in Biur Yesh Omrim seif 1, and will explain below seif 5:`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `And if he did not do so the Acharonim elaborated, and see Peleti, essence of abbreviation; and the conclusion is that if he did not pour the pot but only took with a spoon the eggs and fish from the pot and placed them in a bowl — in this we require 119 fish or eggs, and then all are kosher, and without this all forbidden; but if pouring from pot to bowl the fish with gravy, according to the conclusion, no need for 119 fish, and without this all permitted; but if it became known clearly that the impure fish was at the head of the pot, then need 119 fish, and no need at all for kelipah, even though he also poured gravy:`,
    '1#ב': `Vessels, because we establish vessels on presumption of kashrut — only on this one may question; if so, fish like water; but since it is common to be so in fish in a pot we are concerned, and see Peleti; and one may say that in truth the vessels are permitted after twenty-four hours, for they are in any case absorbed flavor, doubtful rabbinic, and so too Maharshal in responsum:`,
    '2#א': `To return the spoon — even though there is sixty in the spoon against the issur, nevertheless the vessel does not join to nullify the issur, as we say in the Gemara regarding a pot:`,
    '2#ב': `Against the whole spoon, for we do not say regarding a vessel nat bar lichtamei, and see siman 98, which distinguishes between earthenware and metal:`,
    '2#ג': `Against the dish, and the opinion of Shach is against the whole spoon, for we do not know how much was in the spoon and how much it absorbed, and one should be stringent:`,
    '2#ד': `For behold it became nevelah in the spoon, and even though the spoon is a second vessel, since it was inside the pot which is a first vessel and they removed it from there, the heat of the first vessel is still upon it; and regarding a fly, if it imparts taste for spoilage — opinion of Shach in a place where there is no great loss to forbid, and in vinegar and beer even in a place of great loss one should forbid:`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 107 in Taz s.k. 4) See in Shach s.k. 1 — everything is explained correctly:`,
    '2#_': `(There in Taz s.k. 5) And this matter is not clear, etc. According to my humble opinion that case is different, for since there is doubt whether it is that one, the issur in the egg is not known with certainty, lest it is of heter, unlike here where in any case there is issur in the mixture; and without this also according to my humble opinion one should not compare these doubts to one another, for we find many rabbinic doubts to be stringent, as written in Shach end of seif 110:`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `And if he did not do so. Shach end of s.k. 1 wrote that that which fish brine of impure fish is rabbinic — that is specifically through salting and not through cooking; and see what he wrote from this above siman 83 s.k. 5 in name of responsum Noda BiYehudah:`,
    '2#_': `The custom is widespread. See in responsum Radbaz, the new ones, siman 47:`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#_': `And if he did not do so, etc. In Tur authors' disputes are mentioned until the Acharonim elaborated greatly on this with much length and pilpul — the eye of the examiner would weary to reach Tur's conclusion; therefore I thought to clarify the matter briefly and include all their opinions herein, and what to add or subtract as seems to me; and this is in Semag and Tur and Turei Chayim general rule 51, who wrote in name of Semag: if many fish were cooked and an impure fish was found in the bowl — even though there is sixty in the bowl there is dispute between Ri Ba and Rabeinu Baruch whether there is concern lest the impure fish remained with the minority of fish in the pan and forbids others afterward, and likewise in eggs, etc.; and opinion of Rabeinu Baruch is not to forbid; nevertheless he wrote one should be stringent in eggs for it is not customary to cook many together, etc.; and regarding practical halachah it seems initially one should be stringent like Ri Ba; however b'dieved one may be lenient like words of Rabbeinu Shimshon who wrote reason to permit because we rely the issur is in the majority, etc., and in this the Acharonim pilpuled; and it seems certainly if a fish or egg is in the pan and he does not pour it into a bowl but only takes with a spoon the fish and eggs from the pot one after another and places them in a bowl — in this all the concern is perhaps the impure one lies at the bottom of the pot, and if so when he took the fish from the pot and gravy was at the end and there remains only that impure fish with 119 pure fish and no more — if so there is not sixty, for gravy conveys taste from fish to fish and there are no more fish to assist in nullifying it, and they became forbidden in this manner; certainly one should be stringent, for even R"SH who is lenient here agrees his whole reason to permit is because issur is in the majority, and here on the contrary if we say the issur was at the lip of the pot — that is the majority, for most fish were there and he took them with one spoon one by one and removed them outside, and the main point for stringency we say — certainly whatever separates from majority is presumed separated, and that he took with the spoon the fish and removed them outside — mostly he took those that are kosher until the issur remained at the end, as below siman 110, and we trapped them that they move — the Acharonim are forbidden because the issur is at the end (and words of Peri Chadash here are ambiguous); and if these 119 fish afterward mixed with fish in the pot — if among everything there are 119 pure fish, all permitted, for these 119 are nullified in majority which is sixty pure fish; but less than everything forbidden even according to Mechaber who does not hold in other issurim chadichah na'aseh nevelah — nevertheless the 119 fish that became forbidden through emission of impure fish are forbidden forever, for it is impossible to squeeze out the issur and they need nullification in majority; but when there are here 119 fish all permitted and we do not say these 119 fish forbade others through their emission, since there is no gravy there is no absorbed issur going from piece to piece without gravy; but if he poured from pot to bowl the fish with gravy as the order of pouring — if we know the impure fish was at the bottom of the pot there is no issur concern here, for he pours from below, bottom of pot upward according to the pot — if so if there is gravy at bottom of pot to convey absorbed from impure to 119 pure, how much more if there is gravy above it toward mouth of pot — if so all fish above join in nullification through gravy that conveys absorption in all of it, and if so issur is only concern to the contrary perhaps at mouth of pot lies the impure fish, and when he pours from below upward — if so all fish afterward in the row near mouth of pot already the gravy spilled and was poured to bowl and at the end there is gravy only on fish placed at mouth of pot and there lies impure fish and conveys the absorbed gravy to 119 pure fish, and fish below it cannot join in nullification because there is no gravy, and behold these 119 fish became forbidden; and if these 119 fish mixed with fish in the bowl, need also 119 fish so that 119 are nullified in majority as above; only there is another concern here, for he pours fish with gravy — if so gravy conveys taste of absorbed issur within the 119 forbidden fish as above to all fish in the bowl; gravy comes from pot, first vessel, and was poured on what is in bowl, second vessel, and it is pouring, and pouring we rule cooks and absorbs kedei kelipah — if so all fish peel became forbidden; and for this opinion of Shach and Peri Chadash that in truth all fish in bowl are forbidden only the emission on body of fish but kelipah is required (and that is for one who says chadichah na'aseh nevelah in other issurim, and if so all 119 forbidden fish forbid others; but according to Mechaber who does not say chadichah na'aseh nevelah, if so need only against emission of body of impure fish, and in this clearly kelipah of many fish in the bowl with joining will be sixty against the fish; and see Orach Chayim siman 447 regarding olives, etc.) — if so also opinion of Rabeinu Baruch agrees the peels are forbidden — what is this that he troubled to permit the forbidden fish that do not forbid others by touch since absorbed does not go from piece to piece without gravy — for fish from here in any case gravy forbids the peels and more than kelipah without this they do not forbid for it is only pouring; but perhaps for Maharshal who holds solid lump is judged like first vessel — if so even they are solid lump and forbid all fish as first vessel; but cannot be according to Rama who does not hold solid lump; also difficult if opinion of Rabeinu Baruch that peel of all fish forbidden how did he remain silent so much and did not explain, and how did he not infer stringently; therefore it seems the gravy poured from pot to bowl does not pass through all fish in the bowl but separately only first comes on the upper row placed at head of bowl and afterward goes down on fish below it — and if so they did not become forbidden, for the stream was interrupted, only the upper row became forbidden and mixed, and since they became forbidden only through pouring they do not forbid their mixture as we establish something forbidden only kedei kelipah through pouring and mixed does not forbid its mixture whether moist or dry; therefore Rabeinu Baruch was not concerned only in forbidden fish properly there is concern, for in solid lump interrupted stream does not apply as written at length and fish roll in the bowl back and forth, and especially according to Shach that kelipah standing separately and not mixing like a bowl in which they salted forbids mixtures — these fish see Shach siman 69; and my heart tells me in pouring from pot to bowl fish and gravy separately regarding gravy there is interrupted stream, for fish interrupt between first stream and it is not called pouring, unlike the fish themselves where this does not apply — therefore Rabeinu Baruch did not trouble to permit because absorbed issur does not go out without gravy — end of matter; and in this law Rabeinu Baruch holds for the fish themselves need 119 fish as above — on this Maharash disagreed and holds we do not establish presumption of kashrut and we say issur is in the majority; and Rav Peri Chadash challenged Gemara words against each other — that which we say in chapter of mixtures a ring of idol worship mixed in a hundred and they separated forty to one place and sixty to one place — forty does not forbid and sixty forbids, for they say issur is in the majority, and afterward we say there jars of terumah mixed in a hundred jars and the hundred that were nullified in their regard those fifty remaining are forbidden and we do not say issur is in the majority; and both are halachah and they challenge each other; and Peri Chadash resolved to distinguish if they remained in their fixed place that is when we say so, but if they separated we say whatever separates from majority is presumed separated, and through this he challenged Maharash, for here he separated from pot and we do not say issur is in the majority; and in truth there is no place for his words, for certainly all these sayings both deal when they are in fixed place and did not separate, for even though it teaches separated it deals when they separated before us and it is not called separated only fixed as if they did not separate at all, and so Tosafot and Rashba in Torat HaBayit, and see below in Tur siman 110 what he wrote on this; and in truth there is no contradiction at all, for that which we say sixty forbids because issur is in the majority — that is to be stringent, lest you say it is double doubt whether issur was within sixty or not, and if you say it was, perhaps this that mixed afterward in the second mixture there is no issur ring — therefore we say issur is in the majority and if so the first doubt is not in category of doubt; and likewise terumah mixed in a hundred jars we do not be lenient to say issur is in the majority, for to be lenient we do not say, but to be stringent we say, and requires study; and still challenge at first glance proof of Maharash from that which we say issur is in the majority that it helps initially for stringency we say but not to be lenient, and he said to be lenient — this is not so, for it seems he followed words of Rashi who wrote forty does not forbid if they mixed in others but those forty are forbidden and we do not say issur is in the majority because idol worship is not nullified in majority; behold Rashi holds the rule of law is to be lenient and permit the forty and say issur is in the majority only that idol worship is not nullified in majority; and that which we do not rely on majority regarding jars and to permit the fifty that were not opened — one must say it is not specifically idol worship that is not nullified in majority, likewise all issurim that are not nullified we do not say issur is in the majority, and there jars are important matter that is not nullified like idol worship; and if so Maharash holds in our case, and when we judge on cooked impure fish that even in a thousand it is not nullified — that is recognizable, only we judge on its emission whether it was in small amount and not nullified or in majority and nullified — if so this is matter that has nullification and not comparable to idol worship, and well we say issur is in the majority; but all this is superfluous and borrowed language R"SH spoke issur is in the majority — he should have said great principle in Torah whatever separates from majority is presumed separated, and this that he pours fish from pot to bowl all mixed fish and afterward this applies to say on everything whatever separates from majority is presumed separated and they are pure until impure remained at end as below siman 110 and as above, and plainly permitted; and some say here he separated before us and in hands — if so no longer applies whatever separates from majority, and it is fixed — this is not so, for we establish below siman 110 like Rashba that if separated before the doubt arose even separated by hand and before us nevertheless it is called separation from majority is presumed separated, and if so here it was not known from impure fish until afterward, and if so we say whatever separates, etc.; and clear this was not hidden from Rabeinu Baruch only he holds like opinions of his teacher R"Y who holds even before doubt arose nevertheless separated before us it is fixed; and see below siman 110 — if so according to what we establish like Rashba to distinguish between before and after doubt became known, certainly one may be lenient; and behold Rama's opinion also to be lenient because it is like nishpakh where we rely to be lenient, and Acharonim answered we do not say so except in min b'mino where Torah law is nullified in majority, but in min she'eino mino which is entirely Torah law we do not say so, and in this impure fish in pure fish is min she'eino mino, and Shach elaborated on this that in cooking certainly forbidden from Torah and not comparable to brine; and what seems to uphold Rama's words that these are pieces of issur that fell into pot — if so certainly it emitted and afterward fell into another pot there is doubt whether we say certainly it already emitted once and no longer emission here, for it will not emit again, or from doubt it does not emerge lest perhaps it emits now, and this doubt they uprooted in Tosafot in chapter Gid HaNasheh regarding udder that the Gemara challenges "rather it fell into another pot it should not forbid" — Tosafot wrote two answers, either it is doubt and rabbinic doubt for leniency, or certainly we establish for the Sages that certainly it will not emit again, and if so also here from the beginning the impure fish was among many fish in pot full of gravy — if so certainly emitted all possible and afterward when fish and gravy diminished and we come to judge second time whether it will return and emit to forbid — according to one answer no concern for second emission as found in the wagon that we say certainly emitted and will not add to emit more (and to distinguish between vessels and foods is forced), or at least from doubt it does not emerge, and I already wrote above end of seif 99 we do not say on one piece that it emits and returns and emits — if so likewise here; and if so in our case one may permit through double doubt whether impure fish was alone until it returns and forbids or not, only it was in middle of dish, and if you say it was not in middle lest it did not return and emit and there is place of heter — only this is if we do not say nat bar lichtamei, but for us who say nat bar lichtamei — if so this doubt is strengthened, for even though truly it emitted all emission it returned and absorbs from what is in pot and nat bar lichtamei, and afterward when it is with 119 fish it will emit and become forbidden; and if so all concern that impure fish absorbs from what is in pot and all that is in pot is from pure fish, and behold this with pure fish is min b'mino, for absorbed is also taste of fish; and even though we establish meat that absorbed water the water is judged like meat — that is regarding min b'mino not nullified because it does not depend on sixty at all; but in this the difference between its kind and not its kind is that taste is like essence Torah law, and if cooked in it absorbed only from its kind — behold there is no taste like essence and all is judged as min b'mino and according to all Torah law permitted only rabbinic, and in nishpakh we follow lenient view, for all concern that we are stringent is that impure fish absorbed from pot and nat bar lichtamei and this emits and is absorbed in pure fish, and especially since chadichah na'aseh nevelah in other issurim also rabbinic — investigate; and that which we do not permit because of establishing fish on their presumption that they were kosher and we say the impure fish was not in forbidding place — and like cheeses above siman 81 that we say afterward it became tereifah to permit retroactively — and even here one may say thus, since nature of fish and eggs is to turn over in pot through stirring and the like it is common matter and presumption applies to it; and proof for this from that which we say regarding foreleg in Shiloh in Chullin — this is heter that comes from issur principle; Tosafot explained sometimes part of foreleg and part of ram outside gravy, and in tractate Avodah Zarah Tosafot explained the opposite that foreleg and little of ram inside gravy until there is not sixty and Torah permitted; and difficult how we establish presumption of kashrut we place the ram on presumption of kashrut that it was not so — certainly one does not see meat in pot, for if one sees plainly forbidden; for from Torah to permit issur mixture and therefore one does not see — nevertheless we are concerned, learn from this we do not say establish its presumption since it is customary to turn over frequently and therefore we are concerned, and so here:`,
    '2#_': `In chadichah na'aseh nevelah in the spoon, and even though the spoon is a second vessel, nevertheless it was in the pot which is a first vessel and heat of first vessel is still upon it; and I did not understand at first glance if he removed a large piece and it has sides from first vessel — nevertheless we establish not like Maharshal that solid lump is only pouring; and what he wrote large piece and what he wrote spoon that heat of first vessel is upon it — requires study at first glance; and what seems to resolve it is that certainly hot into cold, lower prevails because cold cools hot — therefore if he poured from first vessel to second vessel the cold sides of second vessel cool heat of first vessel, and therefore even in solid lump that has heat from first vessel when it reaches second vessel the second vessel cools it and it is also only second vessel; but that spoon that they removed from first vessel and is still in hand and was not put at all in second vessel — heat of first vessel is still upon it because it was not cooled by anything — therefore it has heat of first vessel — end of matter; and if so likewise solid lump that they removed from dish and before it was placed in second vessel issur fell upon it absorbs in heat of first vessel, for it was not yet cooled in second vessel; and in this will be understood more what we wrote above siman 105 in resolving Gemara words in chapter of mixtures that teaches one who cooked in it and one who poured into it boiling requires purging and rinsing, and we asked suspend it in air of oven, etc., and it resolved from the mishnah that teaches one who cooked in it and one who poured into it boiling — and we already challenged why it calls it so, since pouring does not cook how can there be cooking without absorption in mishnah, and we wrote on it resolution, and in this it will be sweetened more, for it seems in my humble opinion pouring is entirely like second vessel and does not absorb at all, only the piece that is poured into the vessel before the piece reaches sides of vessel heat of first vessel is still upon it and cooks within the piece the moisture and fat and gravy in the piece itself, and behold it is in air of vessel — and the question was resolved and the Gemara dismissed that it is absorption without cooking, for pouring absorbs — end of matter, investigate:`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 107, Shach s.k. 1) And also it is far-fetched that the impure fish. And in a letter that was between me and the Gaon Maharam, may the memory of the righteous be blessed, in Even HaEzer, book Beit Meir, wrote to me a proof that it is called equal in taste, since Rashi wrote (Chullin 97) regarding a pot of meat, and why did he not explain of fish, for it is more common to find an impure fish among pure fish — or because we say for its taste it is stringent, and in a fish among fish it is not so, for they are equal in taste — and this is astonishing:`,
    '2#_': `(Shach s.k. 7) That moment like the pot. And if a hand does not scald in the spoon, see in Peri Megadim (siman 94 in Sha'ar HaTevunah s.k. 8):`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `And if he did not do so, etc., until end of hagahah — in my humble opinion it is proven that Ri Ba and his supporters whom Sefer HaTerumah and Mordechai end of part 9 and Shach siman 51 and Semag (negative commandments 132 daf 44) and Hagahot Maimoniyot and Tur brought — where he poured the gravy first to the vessel and not the fish — nevertheless all are forbidden, for we are concerned lest the impure fish remained at the lower side at the end when the gravy diminished through pouring with a little water and gravy with twenty or thirty fish or more until less than sixty — and if so those twenty or thirty became forbidden even though he poured at the end all the gravy to the vessel, for all those fish outside the gravy on the upper side do not join to nullify since they are outside the gravy, and afterward those twenty or thirty return and forbid by their touch the rest of the fish in the pan that are placed on them in the pan while they are still hot, or that mix in the pan those outside the issur on the upper side with those that were in the minority of water on the lower side at the end of the pouring until they all touch one another — if so all became forbidden; and the opinion of R' Baruch, author of Sefer HaTerumah, and his supporters whom all the poskim brought to that place and agree with his opinion is that even though there is concern lest those twenty or thirty at the end of the pouring became forbidden, nevertheless they are nullified in the majority, since it is customary to have many small fish and there is majority against those that became forbidden, and those that became forbidden do not return and forbid the rest by their touch, since their prohibition is not on account of themselves they do not return and forbid without gravy, as explained in siman 105 seif 7; and he already poured all the gravy at the end to the vessel, and in the bowl there is no gravy, or the gravy in the bowl already became a second vessel and does not forbid at all, as written in siman 105 s.k. 4 — if so they only mixed in dry and are nullified in the majority; but if a little water and gravy remained in the pan the gravy mixes the taste in the whole pan, and in this Hagahot Shach siman 51 that begins deals, therefore in eggs, etc.; and likewise if he did not pour all the gravy and afterward the fish, only poured the fish little by little with the gravy — all forbidden even for R' Baruch, kedei kelipah, for pouring cooks kedei kelipah, as explained in siman 105 there; and so Bach explained, and so is explained in Issur VeHeter general rule 27 see there and investigate; and so is proven from words of Maharshal in his Issur VeHeter in the words "according to when he poured," see there, and so is proven explicitly in Hagahot Shach siman 50 that begins "there was a fish," etc., as he explained see there in all the places and investigate, for I abbreviated, and it is not like Beit Yosef and the Rav in Turei Chayim general rule 51 law 1, who understood the dispute of Ri Ba and R' Baruch when pouring little by little the fish with the gravy to the bowl — except that the Rav in Turei Chayim there wrote (in name of Hagahot Shach siman 51 the aforementioned, and its explanation is not so but as I explained shortly) that even R' Baruch and his supporters do not permit except when he poured the water in the meantime to another vessel after the first pouring before he afterward poured the fish in the pan; but if a little water remained with the fish and he poured them to the bowl — all forbidden, for pouring is like a first vessel, and if so the gravy conveys taste from piece to piece and all forbidden if there is not sixty (and it is difficult for me — why do I need sixty, behold we establish pouring does not absorb more than kedei kelipah, as written in siman 105 s.k. 4, and likewise what does sixty help since the issur does not spread more than kedei kelipah, as is in siman 105 and many places); and in this it does not seem Beit Yosef's opinion is so, but it seems explicitly from his words that even if he did not pour the water in the meantime it is permitted, and it seems to me Beit Yosef holds kelipah in any case we require, and they only disagreed to forbid everything; and so is opinion of Issur VeHeter there seif 6 that even for R' Baruch kelipah nevertheless is required because while still boiling and moist the forbidden touch the permitted see there, unlike Bach who wondered greatly on Beit Yosef and there is no wonder at all; however without pouring Bach permits even without kelipah; and opinion of R"SH whom Semag and Hagahot Shach siman 50 brought who permits because we rely that the issur is in the majority — it seems in any case we require that those on the upper side be the majority, as is in Hagahot Shach the aforementioned; and if so in eggs that R' Baruch forbade because there is no custom to have majority, even for R"SH it is forbidden, and I do not understand what Maharshal chapter 3 siman 44 wrote that for R"SH the eggs are permitted, and so it seems in Turei Chayim there, and here in hagahah however certainly the truth is that for R"SH it is permitted even if he did not pour the water at the end, since we rely the impure fish was in the majority, and so it seems in Turei Chayim there; and regarding practical halachah, behold all these poskim stand in the position of R' Baruch, and likewise Maharif in Tashbetz siman 51 and he brings it in Hagahot Mordechai of Chullin, and likewise Issur VeHeter, and Maharshal agreed there, and likewise Bach and other Acharonim — except that the Rav in Turei Chayim there is lenient in the matter and Bach challenged him and wrote and not like Turei Chayim without reason and proof; and in truth there is to examine the matter, for the Rav's claim in Turei Chayim at first glance is correct, that it is no worse than nishpakh where we follow the lenient view (as explained in siman 98 seif 2), since it is rabbinic and there is doubt whether there is sixty there we follow the lenient view — if so likewise here; and also the second claim is correct, since we establish like Rashba and Tur end of siman 111 that everything enters the doubt — why should not everything here join to nullify; and it is forced to say specifically regarding two pots of heter into one of which issur fell and it is not known to which it fell and in neither is there enough to nullify it — we establish both join since now there is doubt before us and it is not known to which it fell — if so in each one there is to say this is the heter, and even though one of them is certainly issur, nevertheless since it is not known which, both join, unlike here where it was never known which and they mixed together before the doubt arose — on the contrary it is reasonable, for regarding two pots we permit them because whatever is destined to mix we view as already mixed — how much more if already mixed; and it is forced to say all these poskim do not hold like Rashba and Tur, and furthermore if so we should not rule like them since all these poskim disagree with them and especially they are stringent; and furthermore behold Tur also stands in position of R' Baruch and stam in siman 111 like Rashba; and it seems all these poskim deal with min she'eino mino, that impure fish is not its kind, and likewise egg with embryo or egg with a drop of blood is not its kind with other eggs — therefore in min she'eino mino, since it can stand on taste from the beginning, everything does not enter the doubt, for it was possible to come to clarification, as written in siman 92 s.k. 8 and siman 105 s.k. 25, that here also Rashba and Tur agree everything that entered the doubt does not join, and the Rav in Turei Chayim certainly deals only in its kind, as proven from the proof he brought from nishpakh the gravy, that is specifically in its kind, as explained in siman 98; and it seems the Rav understands they deal only in its kind, since they wrote in words of R' Baruch that the fish are nullified in majority as law of dry — and if you think it is min she'eino mino, behold R' Baruch himself wrote in Sefer HaTerumah and poskim below siman 109 that sixty is required because if he cooks them it imparts taste; but in truth this is not proof, for perhaps they always deal not in its kind, and nevertheless the fish in which taste of impure fish was absorbed are nullified in the rest in majority, since they themselves are min b'mino relative to others — if so certainly if he cooks them there is no taste, as written in siman 92 s.k. 8 and siman 105 s.k. 30; and furthermore that certainly egg with embryo or blood in other eggs is min she'eino mino, for taste of embryo and blood separately and taste of egg separately; and also it is far-fetched that impure fish will be equal in taste with pure fish, and how much more according to what the Rav holds in hagahah siman 98 seif 2 that we follow lest — plainly impure fish that has a name by itself is min she'eino mino, as Raavan wrote in responsum siman 20 and Issur VeHeter end of general rule 23 in name of Or Zaru'a; however in this one may say the Rav holds like Hagahot Shach siman 55 that begins once, brought there that impure fish is min b'mino regarding that it is not nullified see there — and requires study, that is because the name of one fish is one, and regarding min b'mino for one who holds it is not nullified we follow the name, as written in siman 98; and perhaps also the Rav even though he understood they deal not in its kind challenged from nishpakh because fish brine of impure fish is rabbinic, as explained in siman 83, and likewise egg with embryo and blood is rabbinic, as explained in siman 86; but it is difficult, for not in its kind even in rabbinic issur the Rav holds we are stringent in nishpakh, as written in siman 98 s.k. 7 — requires study; however according to what I proved there we follow the lenient view — requires study that all these poskim deal when there is fish fatness, for then it is forbidden from the Torah, as explained in siman 83, or the main point is specifically brine through salting is rabbinic, unlike taste through cooking that the essence of taste and moisture comes out, as Maharai wrote in Hagahot Shach siman 20, that impure fish that was pickled with pure fish is forbidden from the Torah, for then the essence of brine and moisture comes out — end of his words; if so how much more through cooking, and that is that no poskim omitted thus regarding cooking that fish brine of impure fish is only rabbinic; and even though Semag and other some poskim mentioned in their language also egg with embryo or blood — they follow their reasons, for Semag holds egg blood is Torah law, as Maharshal wrote in Or Zarua siman 61, or perhaps for example they mentioned thus and it is a difference regarding other Torah issur (and see in Issur VeHeter general rule 26 law 11); and what the Rav wrote in Turei Chayim there and furthermore behold we establish if heter was added, etc. — I already wrote in siman 99 s.k. 15 that it is not so, for we always say chadichah na'aseh nevelah; and furthermore it is not comparable — granted there we say well, such as issur moist mixed in moist or in dry and afterward heter moist was added in a matter where taste is nullified in sixty, unlike here since already forbidden, the other fish do not join to nullify in dry, for issur does not spread from piece to piece without gravy, and it remains in its prohibition in what imparts taste in those twenty or thirty that became forbidden — investigate:`,
    '1#ב': `One should not forbid the vessels, etc. See in responsum of Maharil siman 162, who wrote many reasons to distinguish between food and vessel, and wrote there that it seems he did not permit the vessel except after twenty-four hours, because it is a rabbinic doubt of absorbed flavor, see there; and from this is proof that Semag and the poskim deal with Torah issur, as written in the s.k. before this:`,
    '2#א': `Fly and the like, etc. See end of seif 104, and see what is written in this seif:`,
    '2#ב': `Forbidden to return the spoon to the pot. Even though there is sixty in the spoon against the issur — we do not estimate a vessel with sixty, so Turei Chayim there seif 6, and it was already explained thus above siman 92 seif 5:`,
    '2#ג': `And not against the whole spoon, for we do not say regarding a vessel nat bar lichtamei. And this is specifically in a new wooden or metal vessel; but old or earthenware, even new — opinion of the Rav above siman 98 seif 5 (and see there) that one should be stringent nat bar lichtamei and need sixty against the whole spoon, and likewise Turei Chayim general rule 51 seif 2:`,
    '2#ד': `And it was returned to the pot, etc. It implies that even if it was returned to the pot with the spoon he needs only sixty against the little dish if it is more than the issur nat bar lichtamei, but does not need sixty also against the whole spoon; and so it seems explicitly in Turei Chayim general rule 51 end of seif 2; and it is difficult, since we know there was a little dish with the issur on the spoon — if so who will say to us the whole spoon did not absorb from issur, for even though not chadichah na'aseh nevelah, nevertheless we do not know how much it absorbed, and so Issur VeHeter, and requires study at end of Shach in laws of fly and in Issur VeHeter general rule 27 seif 6 more from this:`,
    '2#ה': `For behold nat bar lichtamei in the spoon. For we maintain that spoon at that moment like the pot itself, since they removed it now from the pot boiling, and it is considered a first vessel for stringency, so Issur VeHeter general rule 27 seif 2; and regarding whether a fly forbids in its taste the Rav agreed to the Mechaber and his supporters — a pity, for behold the majority of poskim, early and late, [forbid], and so Issur VeHeter general rule 27 thus is practiced, and so Maharam Padua thus is practiced, and so Maharshal in Or Zarua and in his book chapter 3 siman 49 from tradition of Maharash thus is practiced; however in a place of great loss or time of pressure and the like it seems opinion of Maharam Padua in responsum to permit, and likewise Bach; however in vinegar and beer for all forbidden, as explained in this seif 104:`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `He should not remove them from the water, etc. Turei Chayim general rule 51 inferred from that Shulchan Aruch did not write that if he did not do so it is forbidden — learn from this that specifically initially he warns, and b'dieved permitted, like those who permit whom Rama mentioned:`,
    '1#ב': `And it forbids again everything in the bowl. So one must say, and Issur VeHeter wrote general rule 27 that if he did not pour them to the bowl but they remained in the pot and he also poured the boiling ones from them — forbidden, lest the forbidden egg was at the bottom of the pot and the eggs near it became forbidden; for until now when the water was conveying taste to all the eggs they all assisted in nullifying, and now that they are not there the other eggs do not assist in nullifying; and since through the moisture of water on them they forbid in any case also the others that afterward touch that which touched the forbidden eggs — therefore they are all forbidden, even if there are a hundred — end of his words:`,
    '1#ג': `And he did not pour them all at once into the bowl, etc. And if there is doubt in the matter whether he poured at once — it seems from Tur to be stringent, and so Issur VeHeter wrote:`,
    '1#ד': `And some permit, etc. The reason Rama wrote here we do not establish issur, etc. is the reason of Rabbeinu Shimshon whom he brings in Semag; and accordingly even in eggs they are permitted. But there another reason for permission is stated, and so in Tur — that is, he permits in fish because those few fish that became forbidden in the pot are nullified afterward in majority mixture and do not return and forbid the rest; and according to this one should forbid in eggs, since it is not customary to cook eggs so much together that twenty or thirty eggs that became forbidden are nullified in majority mixture of heter; and so explicitly in Semag; accordingly there is also a practical difference in the fish themselves — for the second reason Hagahot Shach siman 51 wrote specifically when he poured the water before he poured the last fish remaining in the pan; but if water or gravy remained with a little fish at the end and he poured them to the bowl — all forbidden, for pouring is like a first vessel — end of his words; and according to the first reason there is no distinction in this, and Rashal wrote chapter 3 siman 44 that since Tur mentioned the second reason we establish like him, and one should be stringent in eggs; and accordingly one should be stringent also in the second practical difference I mentioned in the fish themselves — except Rashal wrote there that the water in the bowl does not forbid all the fish through the mixture, since they are in a second vessel; and that little water that remained in the pot also has no power to emit and absorb in the fish in the bowl; and also some say that little water itself that became forbidden at the end of the pouring will forbid the fish in the bowl because it was nullified in the bowl in sixty — end of his words; accordingly if there is concern there is not sixty against that water that remained in the pot, certainly one should forbid all the fish afterward; but opinion of Rama is to rule like the first reason, which is opinion of R"SH that we do not establish issur at all to say the issur was at the end and imparted taste; and Turei Chayim wrote and furthermore since there is to be uncertain in the matter we follow the lenient view, for it is no worse than if we were uncertain in nishpakh the gravy that wherever there is sixty to nullify it in its kind in majority it would only be rabbinic doubt and we follow the lenient view — end of his words; and this is not correct, for since the doubt is whether there is Torah issur here we say it is as certainly Torah issur and as if the issur were clearly at the bottom in the pot — it turns out you have no advantage except in what was nullified one in two, and still there is rabbinic issur and sixty is required, unlike nishpakh the gravy where there are two advantages — one nullification in majority which is from the Torah, and the second nullification in sixty rabbinic; and since there was doubt in nullification which is rabbinic we follow the lenient view in it; and this matter is a general principle in many places that doubt of Torah issur is not considered permitted on account that you have nullification in majority, as we wrote in siman 66 seif 4; and furthermore it is difficult for me here — an egg with embryo or blood is min she'eino mino, for the issur is not its kind with the eggs; and in Hagahot Shach wrote in name of Semag we establish the pan on its presumption and we do not establish issur to say the issur remained at the end, and wrote on this and it seems to me that that which we do not establish on presumption regarding doubt whether he salted slaughtered meat with nevelah or doubt whether there was sixty against the issur not in its kind — that is like legs to the matter — end of his words — evidently also in eggs here there is doubt of Torah issur in any case; and furthermore Turei Chayim brought proofs that everything should join to nullify the issur — I did not know what this applies here, for in eggs we say it is not customary to cook so many eggs that there is majority among everything against those forbidden, and so it seems from words of those who permit in Tur that wrote they are nullified in majority of heter — it seems specifically majority we require; therefore it seems to be stringent like opinion of Rashal that in eggs forbidden and in fish permitted as long as it is possible there is sixty against the little water that remained in the pot. In that matter of fish mentioned in Tur — Beit Yosef's and Rama's explanation is he poured the fish from the pan to the bowl and we are concerned lest the impure one remained there, as written above, and so seems the explanation in Hagahot Shaarei Dura that I brought above, and so in words of Rashal chapter 3 siman 44; and it is wondrous according to this what Tur wrote "the rest in the pan" — he should have said in the bowl; and one must force that Tur deals where the fish remained in the pan only the gravy was poured, and there is concern lest the impure one remained at the bottom of the pan, as written above in name of Issur VeHeter; and regarding practical halachah it is one matter, and Maharach challenged Beit Yosef's explanation that one should forbid what is in the bowl through the pouring that came from the pan, and I already wrote in name of Rashal shortly that there is no difficulty. {Nekudot HaKesef: see there.}`,
    '1#ה': `That we do not establish issur, etc. I already wrote opinion of Rashal that we do not establish so, and so is primary to be stringent in eggs; and even according to this opinion one should be stringent if he cooked many eggs in a pot and among them was one that is forbidden and he removed each one with one spoon — concern lest he removed the forbidden one first and the spoon became forbidden and consequently the others afterward; for that which we say we do not establish issur, etc. — that is because we say the issur is in the majority, and so Turei Chayim quoted in name of R"SH who is master of this opinion, unlike in this law. And likewise Issur VeHeter general rule 51, and wrote there further in this law that even though there is no embryo in the forbidden egg only blood, and egg blood is rabbinic and rabbinic doubt for leniency — that is specifically before the issur became known with certainty in the egg; but after the issur became known with certainty once, one should not distinguish between rabbinic doubt and Torah issur doubt, for whatever the Sages enacted like Torah enactment is enactment — end of his words. {Nekudot HaKesef: see there.} And this matter is not clear, for at the beginning of Beitzah we challenge one who explains the reason of issur of an egg laid on Yom Tov from a decree rabbinic from that which is taught and a doubt is forbidden, for it is a rabbinic doubt and for leniency; and if like words of Issur VeHeter he did not challenge anything, for perhaps he says thus that certainly we have a forbidden egg, only there is doubt whether it was exchanged with another or not — and in this we do not follow the lenient view according to words of Issur VeHeter, but rather that also in such a case it was rabbinic doubt and for leniency; and regarding the main law that Issur VeHeter wrote we establish egg blood is rabbinic — it was already explained in siman 66 seif 4, ruling of Rama that wherever one in two is nullified we rely on one who says egg blood is rabbinic; but without this we are stringent like one who says egg blood is Torah law — therefore here that one in two is nullified, for we recognize the forbidden egg, and therefore even in an egg forbidden on account of blood one should forbid all of them if he took them with a spoon each by itself from the pot — for it is Torah doubt lest the spoon became forbidden first, as seems to me for practical halachah:`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 107 seif 1) One who cooks many eggs in their shells should not remove them from the water until they have cooled. Because we are concerned lest he find an embryo in one of them; and if he were removing from them — perhaps the one that remained, etc., with the last ones and would forbid them because there would no longer be sixty there to nullify it — one must say it is not specifically sixty, for sometimes sixty-one is required, as written siman 86 and siman 98 seif 7; and even though for Mechaber Shulchan Aruch who does not hold nat bar lichtamei in other issur as written siman 92 and end of siman 106 — if so the eggs that remained in the pot do not become nevelah, and when he pours everything to the bowl with sixty against the embryo suffices — nevertheless the eggs themselves become forbidden, as written end of siman 106 that the piece that became forbidden does not return to permission for all; and if so need that there be majority of permitted eggs against them to nullify them as law of dry in dry end of siman 109; and this is clear opinion of Shulchan Aruch; however opinion of Tur requires study, for according to end of siman 106 even the piece that became forbidden returns to permission — if so why did he write this law that follows Semag who holds nat bar lichtamei in other issurim — if so need sixty against all eggs that became forbidden; but Tur who holds we do not say nat bar lichtamei in other issurim and need not estimate except against the first issur absorbed in the piece — and when there is sixty also the piece returns to permission; and if so even if the embryo remained at the end and forbade the eggs that remained there — nevertheless when he afterward pours everything to the bowl, if there is only sixty against embryo all the eggs should be permitted; and I did not reach the depth of Tur's opinion on this — requires study. I again found in responsum Givat Shaul siman 98 who wrote according to Tur that even though he does not hold nat bar lichtamei in other issurim, nevertheless the issur is not nullified in sixty, for perhaps the issur does not spread through hot touching hot in dry only to those touching nearby and we do not know how many they are — see there:`,
    '2#_': `(There in hagahah) And some permit in every case that we do not establish issur to say the issur remained without sixty, and so is primary. And according to this reason it is permitted in every case even if water remained in the pot and he poured them on the eggs in the bowl; and in Tur it states another reason, that those twenty or thirty fish that became forbidden are nullified in majority mixture as law of dry in dry; and likewise Gemara chapter 3 daf 41 regarding worm found in a spit; and one should not challenge, for we establish end of siman 109 in hagahah that dry in dry nullified in majority is in its kind, but not in its kind sixty is required since if he cooks them it imparts taste; one may say impure fish and pure have one taste; however Shach wrote they are not equal in taste; and see in Be'er Yaakov who challenged him from sugya of chapter 3 regarding that which is challenged regarding brine and Ari — min b'mino not nullified, for according to Abaye we follow taste, impure and pure are two kinds; and see Tosafot Menachot (22b) and so is proven explicitly in Gemara and Rif and Rosh and end of chapter 3 (daf 100) that impure fish and pure are nullified in majority dry in dry where it is not a beriah and piece worthy of honor, and here the fish forbidden because they received taste of issur do not have law of beriah as Tur says; and see end of siman 101; and regarding eggs one may say embryo its issur is rabbinic as written siman 86 seif 8; and in rabbinic even not in its kind nullified in majority dry in dry, for even if he cooks them and imparts taste it cannot come to Torah issur as written siman 109 Shach and Be'er Heitev there s.k. 8 and siman 69 s.k. 47; and behold according to this reason one should not permit except in dry but not when he poured hot from pot to bowl, for then through gravy the issur absorbed in fish goes to other fish — it is not dry in dry as all this is explained in Darkei Moshe see there; and in my humble opinion requires study, for in Tur and Beit Yosef it is proven explicitly that even if he poured hot water nevertheless it is dry in dry, for absorbed issur does not return and absorb in dry only through cooking; and Beit Yosef wrote his intent is through actual cooking and not through water that is poured; and it is wondrous, for we establish pouring emits and absorbs at least kedei kelipah as Taz wrote siman 98 seif 10; and answer after my writing I found this answer in Taz and Shach in name of Bach and their answers are forced as understood to one who examines their words; and it seems to me clearly opinion of Tur and Beit Yosef is like Issur VeHeter and hagahah above siman 95 seif 3 that if he poured from first vessel on vessels of meat and milk they do not become forbidden, for pouring is not like first vessel to make the vessels absorb from one another — end of his words; and that is even pouring where stream was not interrupted, for behold similar to what is written there that if he poured from meat vessel on milk vessel it is forbidden, dealing certainly where stream was not interrupted as is explicitly in Shach there and end of seif 94 and end of seif 98; if so if regarding vessels we say so that we establish absorbed issur in vessel goes out even without gravy as written siman 105 seif 7 in hagahah — if so a fortiori two pieces of issur that we establish absorbed issur in piece does not go from it to absorb in another without gravy, for pouring does not make this emit and that absorb; and I again saw explicitly Taz wrote end of seif 98 that Shulchan Aruch holds pouring does not emit and absorb as one; and so Taz wrote end of seif 95; and on this reason one may rely to permit here even if he poured water from pot to bowl; and Rama wrote in Darkei Moshe that custom is to permit in every case because of reason written in hagahah we do not establish issur, etc., and in my humble opinion as written; wrote in Hagahot Mei'iri part 15 from Magen Avraham in name of R"Y that nevertheless need not kasher the pan, for on account of this doubt need not be concerned they remained at end, only establish the pan on presumption of kashrut that it was from the beginning — end of his words, and brought in Turei Chayim:`,
  },
};
